  <section id="roi-is-personal">
    <h2>Why generator payback is personal</h2>
    <p>Financial payback depends on uncertain future outages and on what a homeowner would actually lose without backup power. A standby system also has costs beyond its equipment price: installation, fuel during operation, scheduled service, parts, and eventual repairs. Some homeowners value automatic backup, heat, water, communications, or continuity for work/health needs even when a strictly financial payback cannot be established.</p>
    <p>We do not publish a universal payback period, resale premium, or guaranteed return. A contractor’s quote and the homeowner’s own outage records are better inputs than a generic online “ROI” figure.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="reliability-context">
    <h2>Outage data is useful context—not a household forecast</h2>
    <p>The U.S. Energy Information Administration reported that U.S. electricity customers averaged <strong>11 hours of interruptions in 2024</strong>; major events including Hurricanes Beryl, Helene, and Milton accounted for <strong>80% of the hours without electricity</strong> that year. EIA also reported that the 2024 national average was nearly twice the annual average in the previous decade.</p>
    <p>That is one national year affected by major storms, not a prediction for a particular address or a stable annual average. EIA distinguishes <strong>SAIDI</strong> (average interruption duration) from <strong>SAIFI</strong> (frequency). Local utility performance, weather exposure, and individual service territory matter. Use EIA’s data for broad context and utility reports for local planning; do not infer your personal future outage hours from a national figure.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="costs-to-record">
    <h2>Record the household-specific costs and exposures</h2>
    <p>For several past outages, write down only costs you can substantiate, such as receipts, bills, or documented lost work. Keep direct out-of-pocket costs separate from estimates and from risks that did not actually occur.</p>
    <div class="not-prose mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-[#d9e0ea]">
      <table class="mobile-card-table min-w-[680px] w-full border-collapse text-left text-sm">
        <caption class="sr-only">Static household outage-cost record worksheet</caption>
        <thead class="bg-[#edf3fb] text-primary"><tr><th scope="col" class="p-3">Outage date/duration</th><th scope="col" class="p-3">Documented direct cost</th><th scope="col" class="p-3">Uncertain/indirect impact</th><th scope="col" class="p-3">Source or note</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-200">{Array.from({ length: 5 }).map(() => <tr><td class="h-12 p-3">&nbsp;</td><td class="p-3">&nbsp;</td><td class="p-3">&nbsp;</td><td class="p-3">&nbsp;</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-4">Separately document possible concerns—such as heat, water supply, medical equipment, sump-pump operation, remote work, or food storage—and discuss them with the relevant professional. Do not assign a monetary value to health or safety without appropriate expertise.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="nonfinancial">
    <h2>Non-financial reasons may matter more than payback</h2>
    <p>For some households, the decision is primarily about comfort, accessibility, a well pump, continuity for critical equipment, or confidence during long outages. Those needs deserve careful planning, but no backup system eliminates every outage-related risk. Medical-device backup and runtime requirements should be discussed with the clinician and device manufacturer; do not rely on this publication for medical decisions.</p>
    <p>Also consider noise/placement constraints, local fuel availability, maintenance access, safe installation, and who can respond to service needs. Our <a href="/comparisons/standby-vs-portable/">standby vs. portable comparison</a> describes the operational differences.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="compare-options">
    <h2>Compare complete ownership scope, not a single price</h2>
    <p>When requesting proposals, compare the exact generator and fuel rating, transfer switch, load-management plan, fuel and electrical work, permits, site preparation, startup, and warranty/service scope. Use current local bids rather than treating published national ranges as a personal estimate. Then account for manufacturer-specified fuel use at the loads you expect, current local fuel prices, maintenance, and service arrangements.</p>
    <p>For published planning context and source qualifications, see <a href="/cost/standby-generator-cost/">our cost guide</a>. It distinguishes manufacturer starting MSRP from installed-system estimates and does not claim a guaranteed return on investment.</p>
  </section>
